1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,812
Pull back the plunger of a
syringe and air rushes in.

2
00:00:03,812 --> 00:00:06,191
Squeeze a balloon and air rushes out.

3
00:00:06,191 --> 00:00:08,779
Climb a hill on a bicycle and you pant.

4
00:00:09,179 --> 00:00:11,346
Your chest works like that syringe.

5
00:00:11,346 --> 00:00:14,093
Your lungs do not suck
in air by themselves.

6
00:00:14,543 --> 00:00:17,332
Aarav was panting after
the college hill climb.

7
00:00:17,332 --> 00:00:19,849
He asked: what pulls air into my lungs?

8
00:00:19,849 --> 00:00:21,860
And how much air can they hold?

9
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:23,879
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:23,879 --> 00:00:28,117
Muscles make your chest bigger, the
pressure inside drops, and air flows

11
00:00:28,117 --> 00:00:29,017
in.

12
00:00:28,543 --> 00:00:31,770
One normal breath moves about
five hundred millilitres.

13
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:36,158
By the end, you will know inspiration,
expiration, the respiratory volumes

14
00:00:36,158 --> 00:00:38,452
and capacities, and how to add them up.

15
00:00:39,102 --> 00:00:41,746
First, the one rule behind every breath.

16
00:00:42,096 --> 00:00:45,439
Air always flows from higher
pressure to lower pressure.

17
00:00:45,439 --> 00:00:47,739
Pressure is how hard the air pushes.

18
00:00:48,189 --> 00:00:52,767
When the pressure inside the lungs is
lower than the air outside, air flows

19
00:00:52,767 --> 00:00:53,667
in.

20
00:00:53,212 --> 00:00:54,593
This is inspiration.

21
00:00:54,593 --> 00:00:56,931
When it is higher, air flows out.

22
00:00:56,931 --> 00:00:58,264
This is expiration.

23
00:00:58,914 --> 00:01:02,663
Let's zoom into the chest to find
the muscles that do the work.

24
00:01:03,013 --> 00:01:05,975
The lungs sit in the
air-tight thoracic chamber.

25
00:01:05,975 --> 00:01:09,581
Below it is the diaphragm, a
dome-shaped sheet of muscle.

26
00:01:10,081 --> 00:01:11,099
Zoom closer.

27
00:01:11,099 --> 00:01:15,128
Between the ribs are more
muscles, the intercostal muscles.

28
00:01:15,128 --> 00:01:18,192
Inter means between,
and costal means ribs.

29
00:01:18,692 --> 00:01:20,033
Zoom to the ribs.

30
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:24,345
When the external intercostal muscles
contract, they lift the ribs and

31
00:01:24,345 --> 00:01:26,046
the sternum up and out.

32
00:01:26,696 --> 00:01:29,634
Now follow one breath in, step by step.

33
00:01:29,984 --> 00:01:30,884
Step one.

34
00:01:30,742 --> 00:01:32,961
The diaphragm contracts and flattens.

35
00:01:32,961 --> 00:01:35,864
The chest gets more
room, from front to back.

36
00:01:36,614 --> 00:01:37,514
Step two.

37
00:01:37,413 --> 00:01:39,995
The external intercostal muscles contract.

38
00:01:39,995 --> 00:01:44,371
They lift the ribs and sternum, so
the chest gets bigger the other way

39
00:01:44,371 --> 00:01:45,271
too.

40
00:01:45,618 --> 00:01:46,518
Step three.

41
00:01:46,442 --> 00:01:49,945
The lungs stretch with the
chest, so their volume goes up.

42
00:01:50,695 --> 00:01:51,595
Step four.

43
00:01:51,492 --> 00:01:55,396
The pressure inside the lungs drops
below the air pressure outside.

44
00:01:56,146 --> 00:01:57,046
Step five.

45
00:01:56,987 --> 00:01:59,820
Air rushes in until the
pressures are equal.

46
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:01,202
That is inspiration.

47
00:02:01,952 --> 00:02:02,982
Remember this.

48
00:02:02,982 --> 00:02:05,888
Bigger chest, lower
pressure, air flows in.

49
00:02:06,488 --> 00:02:07,642
Now breathe out.

50
00:02:07,992 --> 00:02:11,038
The diaphragm and the
intercostal muscles relax.

51
00:02:11,038 --> 00:02:15,739
The diaphragm and the sternum go back
to their normal places, and the chest

52
00:02:15,739 --> 00:02:16,751
gets smaller.

53
00:02:17,201 --> 00:02:21,559
The lungs shrink, the pressure inside
rises above the outside, and air

54
00:02:21,559 --> 00:02:22,661
is pushed out.

55
00:02:22,661 --> 00:02:26,619
When we breathe hard, muscles
in the abdomen add extra force.

56
00:02:27,269 --> 00:02:29,315
Let's put the two side by side.

57
00:02:29,665 --> 00:02:30,630
Inspiration.

58
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:34,311
The diaphragm and external
intercostal muscles contract.

59
00:02:34,311 --> 00:02:38,946
Chest volume goes up, lung pressure
goes down, and air comes in.

60
00:02:39,396 --> 00:02:40,296
Expiration.

61
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:41,647
The muscles relax.

62
00:02:41,647 --> 00:02:46,138
Chest volume goes down, lung
pressure goes up, and air goes out.

63
00:02:46,138 --> 00:02:50,044
A healthy person breathes twelve
to sixteen times a minute.

64
00:02:50,694 --> 00:02:51,924
Now, how much air?

65
00:02:52,274 --> 00:02:56,620
A spirometer is a machine that measures
the volume of air breathed in and

66
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:57,520
out.

67
00:02:57,108 --> 00:03:00,219
Doctors use it to check
how well the lungs work.

68
00:03:00,669 --> 00:03:05,216
One normal breath is the tidal volume,
about five hundred millilitres.

69
00:03:05,216 --> 00:03:09,794
The extra you can force in is the
inspiratory reserve volume, two and a

70
00:03:09,794 --> 00:03:11,346
half to three litres.

71
00:03:11,346 --> 00:03:15,863
The extra you can force out is the
expiratory reserve volume, about one

72
00:03:15,863 --> 00:03:16,763
litre.

73
00:03:16,478 --> 00:03:21,096
The air that always stays behind,
even after the hardest breath out, is

74
00:03:21,096 --> 00:03:25,452
the residual volume, about one
point one to one point two litres.

75
00:03:26,052 --> 00:03:26,952
Your turn.

76
00:03:26,883 --> 00:03:30,664
Can the air of the residual
volume ever reach the spirometer?

77
00:03:30,664 --> 00:03:31,887
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:35,087 --> 00:03:35,987
No!

79
00:03:35,542 --> 00:03:40,167
Residual volume never leaves the
lungs, so a spirometer cannot catch it.

80
00:03:40,817 --> 00:03:42,089
Now, a worked example.

81
00:03:42,089 --> 00:03:44,166
Capacities are just
volumes added together.

82
00:03:44,166 --> 00:03:47,605
Take TV five hundred, IRV two thousand
five hundred, ERV one thousand,

83
00:03:47,605 --> 00:03:49,765
and RV one thousand one
hundred millilitres.

84
00:03:50,115 --> 00:03:54,115
Vital capacity is the most air you can
breathe out after the deepest breath

85
00:03:54,115 --> 00:03:55,015
in.

86
00:03:54,512 --> 00:03:56,253
It is ERV plus TV plus IRV.

87
00:03:56,253 --> 00:03:58,193
That is four thousand millilitres.

88
00:03:58,643 --> 00:04:02,906
Functional residual capacity is the
air left in the lungs after a normal

89
00:04:02,906 --> 00:04:03,806
breath out.

90
00:04:03,791 --> 00:04:05,186
It is ERV plus RV.

91
00:04:05,186 --> 00:04:07,360
That is two thousand one hundred.

92
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:12,170
Total lung capacity is all the air the
lungs hold after the deepest breath

93
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:13,070
in.

94
00:04:12,607 --> 00:04:14,563
It is vital capacity plus RV.

95
00:04:14,563 --> 00:04:17,384
That is five thousand
one hundred millilitres.

96
00:04:18,034 --> 00:04:19,495
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:19,845 --> 00:04:23,651
Many students add the residual
volume into the vital capacity.

98
00:04:24,051 --> 00:04:26,952
But vital capacity is
only ERV plus TV plus IRV.

99
00:04:26,952 --> 00:04:29,942
Add RV only when you want
the total lung capacity.

100
00:04:30,592 --> 00:04:31,725
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:31,725 --> 00:04:35,040
As number questions that add volumes
into capacities, and statements on

102
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:36,363
the muscles of breathing.

103
00:04:36,963 --> 00:04:38,247
Exam level now.

104
00:04:38,247 --> 00:04:40,414
Find the inspiratory capacity.

105
00:04:40,414 --> 00:04:41,753
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:47,153 --> 00:04:49,280
The answer is A, three
thousand millilitres.

107
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:54,337
Inspiratory capacity is all the air you
can breathe in after a normal breath

108
00:04:54,337 --> 00:04:55,237
out.

109
00:04:54,833 --> 00:04:56,241
It is TV plus IRV.

110
00:04:56,741 --> 00:05:00,741
Five hundred plus two thousand
five hundred is three thousand.

111
00:05:00,741 --> 00:05:04,740
Four thousand would be the vital
capacity, a common mistake.

112
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:06,880
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:08,463
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:11,663 --> 00:05:13,849
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:14,249 --> 00:05:15,149
One.

116
00:05:14,609 --> 00:05:16,606
What makes the pressure in the lungs drop?

117
00:05:17,506 --> 00:05:18,406
Two.

118
00:05:17,961 --> 00:05:19,333
What is tidal volume?

119
00:05:20,233 --> 00:05:21,133
Three.

120
00:05:20,803 --> 00:05:22,334
What is vital capacity?

121
00:05:23,434 --> 00:05:27,968
Next time: how does oxygen jump from
the air into your blood, and ride

122
00:05:27,968 --> 00:05:29,805
all the way to your toes?

123
00:05:29,805 --> 00:05:32,906
We find out in exchange
and transport of gases.
